/*
Original work, written for tests/fixtures/nonlinear-timeline (temporal-
consistency mechanism material, 2026-08-03). Mixed structure: a FLASHBACK
bracket (returning via CONTINUOUS) plus a separate forward time-jump
using a LATER heading modifier, per the markers
extractTemporalConstraints() actually parses. Temporally clean as
written -- 0 expected contradictions.
*/

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - FRONT HALL - DAY

Moving boxes stacked three high. MARGUERITE OYELARAN, thirties, keys still in hand, stands in the doorway of a house she clearly used to live in.

MARGUERITE
(to herself)
Same creak in the third stair. Nobody fixed it. Good.

EXT. TERRACE HOUSE - BACKYARD - DAY

A small, overgrown garden, a rusted swing set still standing in one corner. Marguerite crosses the grass slowly, stops at the swing, gives it a light push.

MARGUERITE
Still squeaks. Some things this house refuses to let go of.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

She runs a hand along the mantel, stops at a faded pencil mark and a name scratched beside it: height measurements climbing the wall.

MARGUERITE
Twelve years and the landlord never painted over this.

She presses her palm flat against the marks like she's checking her own height against them.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY - FLASHBACK

The same wall, decades younger paint. YOUNG MARGUERITE, eight, stands rigid against it while her FATHER pencils a line just above her head.

FATHER
Hold still. You grow an inch every time you fidget, I swear it.

YOUNG MARGUERITE
I'm not fidgeting, I'm growing on purpose.

He laughs, marks the wall, ruffles her hair.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY - CONTINUOUS

Still the flashback, moments later: Young Marguerite steps back to inspect the new line, deeply unsatisfied with the gap between it and the last one.

YOUNG MARGUERITE
That's barely anything.

FATHER
It's exactly what you earned this year. Ask for more, you get less. That's the rule.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY - CONTINUOUS

Marguerite, adult again, blinks back into the present, still touching the marks. A REAL ESTATE AGENT appears in the doorway behind her, apologetic.

AGENT
Sorry -- didn't mean to startle you. The inspector's running late.

MARGUERITE
(not turning around yet)
Take your time. I'm not in a hurry to leave this wall.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

The agent steps in, clipboard ready, scanning the room with professional detachment.

AGENT
Original crown molding, working fireplace. This place will move fast once it's listed.

MARGUERITE
It moved fast the first time too. We were only meant to stay a year.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

Marguerite runs her fingers along a chipped tile edge, a small private smile surfacing.

MARGUERITE
(to the agent)
There's a burn mark under the stove. Nobody ever tell you why it's there.

AGENT
(checking clipboard)
No burn mark listed in the disclosure.

MARGUERITE
Exactly. We kept it a family secret. Some legacies you protect.

EXT. TERRACE HOUSE - BACKYARD - DAY - THREE YEARS LATER

The same overgrown garden, a fresh coat of paint on the swing set's frame. Marguerite pushes Isabel gently on the swing, both of them mid-laugh.

ISABEL
Higher! You always push too slow.

MARGUERITE
Your grandfather used to say the same thing about me.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY - THREE YEARS LATER

The wall has been repainted, the height marks gone entirely. Marguerite, hair different, more settled, stands with her own daughter, ISABEL, seven, in the same spot her father once stood.

MARGUERITE
Hold still. You grow an inch every time you fidget, I swear it.

ISABEL
I'm not fidgeting, I'm growing on purpose.

Marguerite freezes, pencil in hand, recognizing her own line coming back to her from somewhere she didn't expect it.

INT. TERRACE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

She marks the wall carefully, steps back, studies the fresh line against nothing -- the first mark of a whole new column.

MARGUERITE
(quiet, to herself)
Ask for more, you get less. That's the rule.

ISABEL
What rule? You're not making sense, Mom.

MARGUERITE
(smiling)
You'll understand it in about twenty-five years. I've got time to explain it slowly.

She sets the pencil down on the mantel, in the exact spot where one always used to live.
